  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday night through Monday)\nIssued at 214 AM EDT TUE SEP 1 2026\n\nThe longwave pattern will feature amplified flow across the \nmajority of the CONUS through early next week. Troughing will \ngenerally dominate west of the Rockies. A more anomalous ridge, \ninitially sprawled from Kentucky through the Carolinas into \nThursday, will temporarily dampen and retrograde into the \nPlains/Central CONUS. The ridge center will then strengthen once \nagain as it anchors further southwest over the Central to Southern\nPlains to end the period early next week. Guidance does vary from\nrun to run and model to model over the placement and strength of\nthis feature by the end of the period. However, this general \npattern will allow for the Ohio and Tennessee valleys to be \nsubject to northwesterly flow aloft, with periodic shortwaves \nworking southeast across the region. While model agreement is \nsolid through Thursday to Friday, detail differences bring more \nspread regarding weather specifics by the end of the weekend and \ninto early next weekend.\n\nConfidence remains high for the hot temperatures to continue across \neastern Kentucky through the end of the work week. Highs will remain \nin the mid 90s for most locations on Thursday, potentially again \nnearing daily records on Thursday of 96 from 2011 for both JKL \nand LOZ. Pending the degree of clouds and convection highs may \nnear records again on Friday (94 degrees from 2007 for both JKL \nand LOZ). With the upper level ridge axis overhead, expect the \nassociated subsidence to keep a lid on convection and cumulus \nthrough Thursday. The ridge will lose its grip by Friday, with a \nsurface cold front approaching from the north. Rain chances will \nreturn across the area, mainly in the isolated range, with one \nmore hot and humid day expected for Friday, as temperatures return\nto the lower 90s, coupled with dew points in the lower 70s. As \nthe boundary sags south over the weekend and a couple of \nshortwaves pass, there will be additional chances for convection. \nThese look to peak diurnally on Saturday, though only in the \nisolated to scattered range of coverage, with the front in the \narea and a shortwave passing near peak heating. Low level \nnorthwesterly to northeasterly flow moves in behind the front, \nputting an end to the 90-degree days for the entire area by \nSunday, with highs returning to more seasonable 80s for early \nSeptember. While PoPs look to lower into early next week, the \nlack of surface features and the lingering northwest flow aloft \ncontinues to preclude going completely dry for any given period at\nthis time.",
